 - Bash: git ls-files -z '*.rs' | xargs -0 cat | wc -l; git log --format='%an <%ae>' | grep -iE 'ines|inès|okoro' | sort | uniq -c
FINAL ANSWER:
Yes, it's mostly one person. Andrew Gallant owns about 64% of the surviving Rust lines. I used `git blame -w` on the 10 tracked `.rs` files, which cover 4,366 lines.

| Rank | Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Andrew Gallant | 2,798 | ~64% |
| 2 | Sam Okoro | 646 | ~15% |
| 3 | Inès Moreau (two spellings, see below) | 217 | ~5% |
| 4 | nivkner | 146 | ~3% |
| 5 | Sebastian Thiel | 96 | ~2% |
| 6 | Marvin Löbel | 83 | ~2% |

Below those are mcharsley (46), Aron Griffis (45) and Martin Sehnoutka (38).

- **Moreau:** Git shows "Inès Moreau" with 120 lines and "Ines Moreau" with 97. The commit log has a third identity, `ines <<email redacted>>`. The two spellings use different emails (`ines.moreau@university.example` and `ines@moreau.example`), so I can't confirm they are the same person. I'm assuming they are and combined them to get 217 lines. Taken separately they would rank below nivkner. There is no `.mailmap` merging them.
- **Gallant by commit count:** He has 135 of the commits in the log, far more than anyone else. Sam Okoro has only 2 commits but holds the second-largest share of lines.
- **Whitespace and line endings:** I used `-w` so the "normalize line endings" commit doesn't take credit for every line. Moves and renames are not traced across files, so shares could shift slightly with `-M -C`.

